Your notes are heading in a good direction, but I would make one important architectural change: do not use the OpenAI Batch API for individual ARCH-006 message translations.

You already have Redis/BullMQ providing the asynchronous boundary. For a 500-character support message, the clean flow is:

Merchant/Admin submits message
        ↓
Validate 1–500 characters
        ↓
Persist original message in PostgreSQL
        ↓
Create Translation row = PENDING
        ↓
Enqueue translation job in Redis
        ↓
Background worker consumes job
        ↓
Call translation provider
        ↓
Validate returned translation
        ↓
Update Translation row = AVAILABLE

That gives us asynchronous processing without adding another asynchronous batching layer.

The OpenAI Batch API is designed for offline workloads and has a 24-hour completion window, although jobs often finish sooner. It is useful for thousands of non-urgent requests, but not ideal for a merchant support inbox where an administrator may expect the translation within seconds.

I agree with the 500-character limit — with one distinction

I would change ARCH-006 from:

1..10,000 characters

to:

Merchant-authored message: 1..500
Admin-authored message:    1..500

and enforce it both client-side and server-side.

But I would not limit the translated result itself to 500 characters.

For example:

originalBody
  ≤ 500 user-visible characters

translatedBody
  may exceed 500

Some languages naturally expand significantly during translation. A perfectly valid 500-character English message could become 600+ characters in another language.

So:

500 = author input constraint
NOT translation-output constraint

And we should reject over-length input rather than silently truncate it.

That means I would remove this from your proposed prompt:

If the original message breaks off mid-sentence due to a character limit...

Moda shouldn't deliberately send a chopped message to the translator. The UI should stop the author at 500 and the server should reject anything exceeding the limit.

I would also make "character" Unicode-safe

Do not use simply:

body.length <= 500

because JavaScript counts UTF-16 code units. Emojis and some non-Latin characters can count strangely.

We should define the rule as approximately:

500 user-perceived Unicode characters

and use Intl.Segmenter/grapheme segmentation where supported.

That matters for the global-language work we've just done.

